WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:01.829 align:start position:0%
 
for millennia humans have turned to  plants to heal the sick and wounded to  ward against evil and grant magical  powers but what happens when plants  themselves become conscious and turn  killers more monstrous associations link  vegetation to malevolent sorcery demonic  influence or evil and typically  human-like qualities characteristics  like extremely large size enhanced  mobility including sentient movement an  insatiable carnivorous diet and  poisonous excretions can all make a  harmless plant into a terrifying monster  the most common unifying element of  terror among these so-called monstrous  plants their ability and desire to kill  humans  i'm dr emily zarka and this is monstrum  poisonous plants like hemlock and  belladonna are lethal to humans and have  been employed as such given these  potentially devastating consequences  some of this vegetation was associated  with mystical properties especially if  they also possessed an unusual  appearance orchids were associated with  witchcraft deadly nightshade and  mandrakes with satan these real world  deadly plants helped inspire the  folklore around their monstrous cousins  the ones that killed consciously on  their own one early example appears in  the medieval period in a natural history  encyclopedia published in 1491 maidenbox  ortus sanititis includes a description  and illustration of the bozer tree  it was said to exude toxic fumes that  caused death for miles around for any  living thing even humans some 250 years  later in 1783 an article appeared in the  london magazine by dutch surgeon n p  forsh who told of the existence of a  strange tree the javanese uppas  he alleged that on the island of java  there existed a tree whose biology was  so sensational it had previously been  dismissed as myth by european  naturalists according to forsh  indigenous peoples called this plant the  bohun upas with the permission of the  government and the help of two malayan  priests forsh claims that he was able to  observe the tree himself from a distance  of course as not only the bark of the  tree but the fumes emanating from its  resin are lethal to all life  the only humans who approach the tree  are condemned criminals given a chance  at clemency if they can successfully  harvest the poisonous secretions the  existence of force and his record of the  tree are still contested historical  documents show that a surgeon named john  nichols force did indeed exist and that  the man lived in the east indies  this surgeon expressed interest in  writing a book about his travels so it  is possible he concocted the bohun upa  story as a publicity stunt however other  historians attribute the london magazine  article to an anonymous source who spun  the tale from scratch either way there  is a real tree with poisonous sap we now  call the onitarous toxicaria that was  undoubtedly well known among inhabitants  of its natural habitats  but it wasn't until sensationalized  accounts like this one in the 18th  century that this deadly plant was  turned into a plant that killed many  such stories weren't just tabloid fodder  but were nonetheless just as sensational  naturalist erasmus darwin wrote of the  javanese uh tree and its poisonous vapor  in his 1789 text the botanic garden  offering the compelling image of a  barren land surrounding the plant  punctuated only by the skeletons of men  and animals while not the man-eating  plant trope we've come to recognize as  characteristic of killer flora these  early literary appearances planted  imaginative seeds that would flourish in  the 19th century as the steam engine  advanced and expediated trade routes  transport of plants from one side of the  globe to the other became more  successful in the 19th century  improvements in glass and steel  production used to build greenhouses and  the invention of the hot house made  cultivation of foreign plant species  possible in previously unfit climates  alongside this perennial privilege  plants as intruders was an increasingly  dominant narrative in the 1800s wild and  unkempt at best or sentient and mobile  in the most horrifying variations the  idea of encroaching vegetation appeared  across literary genres fictional travel  accounts increasingly featured exotic  killer plants gothic tales and short  stories of weird vegetation became  popular meaning an influx in the  perception that flora could consume  animal flesh including the occasional  human this is in part due to a  scientific text and another darwin  charles darwin's 1875 book insectivorous  plants popularized the flesh eating  plant  the vibrantly illustrated text details  the naturalists experiments with  carnivorous plants their movements  digestive processes and favorite food  sources  nature it became apparent supported  natural monsters and inevitably fiction  writers would expand upon their  existence in dramatic ways fake accounts  of man-eating plants emerged in  publications around that same decade  perhaps the most widely popularized of  these tales was the man-eating tree of  madagascar on april 28 1874 the new york  world published an incredible story  detailing the account of carl lech's  discovery of a tentacled carnivorous  man-eating pitcher plant in madagascar  he claimed the tree was worshipped by  locals secreted a hallucinogenic sap and  used vine-like tentacles to trap its  victim while pointed leaves squashed  them to death more than 10 years later  in 1888 the story was revealed as a hoax  but by that time fiction writers were  filling in the gaps of imaginations  turning newspaper stories into  sensational horror narratives take  arthur conan doyle's the american's tale  a short story about a giant carnivorous  venus fly trap in montana or phil  robinson's the man eating tree where an  african man sucking tree with beautiful  golden fruit serves as a lure to grab  nearby prey with its boughs before using  large vampire-like leaves to suck the  blood of its victims in lucy h hooper's  1889 carnivorine the mad scientist trope  enters into the genre with a botanist  who attempts to prove an evolutionary  connection between plant and animal  as a result of his experiments the plant  develops paddle-like feet making it  mobile and a taste for human flesh  resulting in an uncontrollable killer  monster and in 1891 the horrible plant  called the devil's snare was introduced  by an american naturalist living in  nicaragua  like a weeping willow without foliage  the thick dark black vines appear like a  mass of interlacing thick muscular  fibers vines that suck blood of course  in 1894 h.g wells the flowering of the  strange orchid tells the tale of a  british orchid collector whose newest  procurement from the indies whose buds  resemble little white fingers ends up  nearly strangling him using  sucker-adorned roots to drink his blood  noticing a trend here  the plants in these narratives come from  outside of europe  many killer plant stories from the 19th  century and beyond represent the  unconquered and uncontrollable natural  world colonialism plays a role here as  writers turned passively poisonous flora  into predatory man-eating beasts  painting the foreign as dangerous and  terrifying as a means to affirm western  superiority at the turn of the 20th  century plants were increasingly  considered with curiosity suspicion and  fear in both scientific texts and horror  fiction the 1920s and 30s saw swell in  monstrous plant narratives  pulp science fiction was thriving at the  time but so were debates about teaching  evolutionary theory in schools ones that  made national headlines with the scopes  trial fostered by the uncertainty of the  cold war and the rapidly evolving  scientific discoveries of the 50s and  60s monster plants popped up regularly  on screen and in books  speculations surrounding the potential  impacts of nuclear mutation  extraterrestrial life bioweapons and  chemical herbicides present themselves  in monstrous plant narratives in the  famous the day of the trifids a  post-apocalyptic 1951 novel by john  wyndham mysterious plants are metaphor  for fears of the cold war when a comet  blinds most of the world's population  humankind becomes vulnerable to walking  stinging plants first bred by the soviet  union during genetic experimentation and  accidentally released into the world  the triffid plants evolved to prey on  humans using the global catastrophe to  dominate the planet that same year in  the film the thing from another world  killer extraterrestrial plants wreak  havoc on earth  inspired in part by growing reports of  ufos and aliens as well as rumored  accounts of soviet forces in the arctic  the film's plant-based alien life form  is defrosted by military personnel and  scientists in the north pole if this  plot sounds familiar that's because it  is both this movie and 1982's the thing  were based on a 1938 novel by john w  campbell jr in 1956 invasion of the body  snatchers would make monstrous plants  more terrifying by giving them the  ability to clone humans then the woman  eater paid homage to the 19th century  sensational mankiller plant narratives  with a carnivorous tree worshiped by  locals and 1960 brought us the hapless  skid row florist seymour who cultivates  a blood thirsty potted plant hoping to  use the novelty to advance his own  standing with his boss and propagate his  own love life in the original 1960s  version of the film audrey jr defeats  and eats seymour audiences of the 1986  version of little shop watch a more  pleasant and reassuring ending when it  is instead seymour who defeats audrey jr  audrey junior immortalizes its victims  faces in its blooming flowers  which the historian of me can't help but  interpret as a callback to the walk walk  tree first recorded in the late 14th  century book of surprises the bizarre  tree appears in multiple indo-arabian  legends where it is said to grow either  human heads or full-bodied humans  amongst its branches in the 1970s and  80s advances in bioengineering led to a  flurry of dangerous flora narratives in  response to anxieties about the  consequences of bioengineering venus  flytrap returns to the premise hooper  first introduced in the 19th century  when a botanist looking to prove human  life evolved from plants accidentally  creates a monster in 1985 super mario's  piranha plants inspired in part by the  real-life invasive plant species  hydronora africana immortalized killer  plants in video games and in 1989 the  plant-based kaiju biollante joined the  godzilla franchise  since the 21st century killer plants in  horror and science fiction narratives  have become more varied in appearance  origin and definition from the more  obvious interpretations creeping  carnivorous vines and anthropomorphic  tree stump babies  to unseen parasitic fungi that cause  humans to crave flesh and psychedelic  alien spores  we still have the tendency to transform  nature into something frightening the  biological systems of plants are  dramatically different from other life  forms most make their own food and  breathe what we exhale as waste it is  more challenging for us to draw  similarities between flora than fauna  even when we try to make comparisons  like with the mandrake large duck orchid  or bleeding heart flowers the analogy is  never fully formed this extreme  otherness can heighten the terror when  they turn monstrous exploiting our  discomfort with living beings whose very  existence is so dramatically different  from our own so next time you gaze  lovingly at the plant sitting serenely  in the corners of your home spilling  leaves over bookcase shelves and  silently swinging from macrame hammocks  remember that it is in the quietness  that seeming meekness that the killer  plant lies ready to strike so have you  talked to your plants today  if you love the outdoors we think you'll  really enjoy the newest digital series  from pbs called america outdoors  understory hosted by baratunde thurston  the show explores our passionate yet  complex relationship with the natural  world along the way you get to meet  everyone from surfing scientists to  fossil hunters there's a link in the  description we hope you go check it out  and then maybe go get some fresh air  rolling what is that  ooh past this one native  plant i really want a venus fly trap as  a pet  okay

